{
	"version": 5,
	"id": "BCvFRi_vBYYPzaKjh",
	"notes": "A Deathmark: a dynasty's sniper and infiltrator, phased partly out of reality between shots. Point math: Necron Physiology 174 + Body Lens: Deathmark 552 + Mind: Deathmark Focus -5 + Deathmark Phase-Shift 18 + skills 28 = 767 points. Stats: ST 26, DX 13, IQ 9, HT 14, HP 32, Will 9, Per 9, natural torso DR 60 (Weak Points 4, eye DR 30) — framework.md's Deathmark anchor line exactly. This lands within framework.md's Astartes tier (550-800): a Deathmark is a dedicated one-target killer whose whole build (DX, stealth and marksmanship skills, minimal social skills) reflects a specialist rather than a line trooper or an all-rounder.\n",
	"traits": [
		{
			"id": "TLu6JFKLiFMAf0Ba4",
			"name": "Necron Physiology",
			"local_notes": "Lore: The Necron Physiology shared by every caste of the species: a body of living necrodermis animated by a soul drawn out of a long-dead Necrontyr and bound into the metal during the biotransference, self- repairing, near-unkillable in the field thanks to Reanimation Protocols, and untouched by disease, poison, pain or the warp's usual tricks. This trait is the baseline every body-type lens below (Warrior, Immortal, Deathmark, Lychguard, Flayed One, Noble, Cryptek) builds on top of; the lens supplies the caste's own attributes and necrodermis DR, and a separate mind package supplies its personality (or lack of one).\n\nDesign: 30 (Injury Tolerance) + 50 (Regeneration) + 43 (Reanimation Protocols) + 10 (No Pain Receptors) + 30 (Resistant to Metabolic Hazards) + 10 (Soulless) + 1 (Beneath a Daemon's Notice) + 0 (Machine-Body flavor) = 174 points of shared physiology before attributes, necrodermis DR, personality or skills. See each template for a full character built on top of this plus a body lens and a mind package.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "ancestry",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tBjFtiHxQ82zS1Frf",
					"name": "Injury Tolerance (Unliving)",
					"reference": "B60",
					"local_notes": "Unliving wound multipliers (imp and pi++ x1, pi+ x1/2, pi x1/3, pi- x1/5); attacks to the vitals or groin are treated as attacks to the torso; does not bleed and is unaffected by blood-borne toxins.\n\nLore: A Necron body is living metal animated by a bound soul, not flesh wrapped around organs — there is nothing inside it to bleed or to target with a called shot to the \"vitals,\" and impaling or small- calibre fire that would drop a man does comparatively little to a chassis with no soft tissue to wound. Consistent across Codex background and both Rath's and Crowley's novels, which repeatedly show las-fire and small arms punching clean through a Necron with no effect beyond cosmetic damage until something heavier lands.\n\nDesign: Injury Tolerance (Unliving) (B60-61): Unliving 20 + No Blood 5 + No Vitals 5 = 30 points. No Brain/No Eyes/No Neck/No Head are deliberately left off: a Necron does have a reanimation-critical core the framework's Weak Points rule targets, and a called shot to the eye-slit is handled through the Necrodermis trait's notes below rather than by removing the eye as a target.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mFWxhLMqmQUNHBMnH",
							"name": "Unliving",
							"reference": "B61",
							"cost_adj": "20"
						},
						{
							"id": "mSDyTrVouvldczMv1",
							"name": "No Blood",
							"reference": "B61",
							"cost_adj": "5"
						},
						{
							"id": "mkoGpjNAo4ij4J6ed",
							"name": "No Vitals",
							"reference": "B61",
							"cost_adj": "5"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tTNj3-eyKQ-APTSkQ",
					"name": "Regeneration (Necrodermis Self-Repair)",
					"reference": "B80",
					"local_notes": "Regeneration (Fast): recovers 1 HP per minute, on top of Reanimation Protocols' recovery from being felled. Nobles, Lychguard, Crypteks and Destroyers upgrade it to Very Fast in their body lens. Heals injury, not lost limbs (Regrowth is a separate advantage).\n\nLore: Necrodermis is living metal: dents smooth out, gouges knit shut and severed plating crawls back together while the Necron keeps fighting, as the tabletop's Living Metal rule and the fiction show (Crowley's Twice-Dead King has Necron bodies reknitting mid-battle). Reanimation Protocols below are the bigger trick, raising a Necron that has actually been felled.\n\nDesign: Regeneration (Fast) (B80, 50 points; user direction, raised from Slow). The tabletop gives Living Metal to characters and war engines while rank-and-file Warriors rely on Reanimation Protocols, so the shared physiology mends at Fast (1 HP per minute: whole between engagements, not mid-firefight) and the Noble, Lychguard, Cryptek and Destroyer lenses buy the upgrade to Very Fast (1 HP per second), which shows inside a fight.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 50
				},
				{
					"id": "t-QQ2ARVJJdeZjk7V",
					"name": "Reanimation Protocols",
					"reference": "B55",
					"local_notes": "When damage reduces a Necron below -1xHP, it is not destroyed outright unless a single injury exceeds -5xHP (the normal instant- kill threshold still applies past that point). Otherwise its body drops, inert. At the end of each of the next three turns, roll HT: on a success the Necron reanimates then and there, rising with HP restored to half its full total (round down, minimum 1) and fully combat-capable. Three failures in a row, or any critical failure, and it phases out instead: teleported home to its tomb complex or the nearest active node in a flash of green light, removed from the fight but not destroyed, and available to return in a later scene at the GM's discretion. A Necron whose remains are physically destroyed past -5xHP, disintegrated, or whose reanimation matrix is separately wrecked (a GM-adjudicated called shot, per the fiction's \"destroy the core\" solution) does not come back at all.\n\nLore: Reanimation Protocols are the signature Necron trick that makes Necron armies so dangerous to fight to a standstill: a Warrior cut down one moment can be back on its feet the next, teleported away only when the protocols themselves fail. Codex background across editions describes exactly this two-stage process (reanimate in place, or phase out to a tomb on failure), and both Rath's The Infinite and the Divine and Crowley's Twice-Dead King show Necrons shrugging off \"kills\" that would end any other soldier, while also showing that a thoroughly wrecked or disintegrated Necron stays down for good.\n\nDesign: Built on Basic Set's Extra Life (B55, base 25 points: you do not permanently die when killed, but eventually return to bodily function). Modified since this fires every time the trigger condition is met over a whole campaign rather than a fixed number of one-shot lives (Recurring, eyeballed at +100%, the same kind of judged percentage this project's Reputation and Accessibility entries use rather than a strict RAW calculation), Delayed Return (-20%, the multi-turn HT-roll window instead of an instant pop-up) and Partial Restoration (-10%, half HP rather than full). Net +100-20-10 = +70%. 25 x 1.7 = 42.5, rounded up to 43 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mtAPw2xEGZOvvaJFt",
							"name": "Recurring (triggers every time the condition is met, not once ever)",
							"cost_adj": "+100%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mtFrwGNebXUCsznmM",
							"name": "Delayed Return (HT roll over several turns, not instant)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						},
						{
							"id": "mL2Xe1K2dwVkPsR3Y",
							"name": "Partial Restoration (returns at half HP, not full)",
							"cost_adj": "-10%"
						}
					],
					"base_points": 25
				},
				{
					"id": "tAfjSmez3e5UcarmQ",
					"name": "No Pain Receptors",
					"reference": "B59",
					"local_notes": "As High Pain Threshold (B59): a Necron's necrodermis has no pain receptors to speak of, so it takes no shock penalty from injury and is not stunned by it.\n\nLore: A Necron does not flinch, wince or slow down from injury short of outright incapacitation — it has nothing resembling a nervous system built to register pain in the first place. Consistently background across Codex material and Black Library's Necron fiction, which never shows a Necron reacting to a wound the way a living creature would.\n\nDesign: Plain Basic Set High Pain Threshold (B59, 10 points). RAW High Pain Threshold already removes shock penalties and the stun that injury would otherwise cause, which is everything a Necron's lack of a pain sense needs; an earlier \"house rule total\" version was redundant.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 10
				},
				{
					"id": "tJoXhLbWIPEmH6Y2z",
					"name": "Resistant to Metabolic Hazards (Necrodermis)",
					"reference": "B81",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Metabolic Hazards), Immunity.\n\nLore: A body of living metal has no metabolism for disease, poison, radiation or asphyxiation to act on, and it does not age in any biological sense — a Necron's chassis wears out mechanically over deep time rather than growing old. Universal, unremarked-on Codex background.\n\nDesign: Resistant (Metabolic Hazards), Immunity tier (B80-81): base value 30 for the broad Metabolic Hazards category, x1 for full Immunity = 30 points, the same base-value component this project's Basic Set \"Machine\" reference build (see the Basic Set library's own Machine meta-trait) uses for the same reason.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m5HOHWTfgB63yld9q",
							"name": "Metabolic Hazards (disease, poison, radiation, suffocation, aging)",
							"reference": "B80",
							"cost_adj": "30"
						},
						{
							"id": "mPrXJelmaw3wWBW1n",
							"name": "Immunity",
							"reference": "B81",
							"cost_adj": "x1"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "te98mNBMdZZQFKQQ8",
					"name": "Soulless",
					"reference": "B80",
					"local_notes": "Resistant (Mind Control, Possession and Emotion Control), Immunity.\n\nLore: Whatever a Necron's soul is now, it is bound into the necrodermis by Necrontyr technology rather than carried the way a living creature's is, and it registers as flatly uninteresting to the predators of the warp: daemons ignore Necrons in the fiction unless given a specific reason not to, and no depiction shows a Necron possessed, charmed or emotionally manipulated by psychic or daemonic means. This is consistent Codex background rather than a specific cited scene.\n\nDesign: Resistant, Immunity tier (B80-81: base rarity value x5), following this project's own Pariah Gene \"Immunity to Warp-Based Effects\" entry (data/imperium/psykers/warp.yaml) for the identical math: Mind Control/Possession/Emotion Control treated as an \"occasional\" hazard category (base 2) for a race this is definitional for, 2 x 5 = 10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "m4lK7QFeesbSVsliz",
							"name": "Mind Control, Possession and Emotion Control simply do not work",
							"reference": "B81",
							"cost_adj": "10"
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "t0YEq4p5Gm6JVi_rU",
					"name": "Beneath a Daemon's Notice",
					"local_notes": "Roleplaying/GM-adjudication flag: daemons default to ignoring a Necron target unless it is actively fighting them or blocking something they want.\n\nLore: A soulless construct is not worth a daemon's attention the way a mortal's soul is: Codex background and both Rath's and Crowley's novels play Necrons and daemons as largely talking past each other or fighting for territory rather than as predator and prey the way daemons treat the living.\n\nDesign: Perk (B21, 1 point): a narrow, GM-adjudicated targeting-priority flag rather than a numeric combat bonus, since \"usually ignored unless it's in the way\" is not the kind of reliable effect Basic Set prices as a full reaction bonus.",
					"tags": [
						"Perk",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "tYNffGH8phjz39k7S",
					"name": "Necrodermis Machine-Body",
					"local_notes": "0-point lore flag, the same convention this project's Ork Fungal Physiology entry uses: a Necron does not need to breathe, eat, drink or sleep, and its chassis wears out over millennia rather than ageing — all inherent to being a machine body under GURPS's own Machine meta-trait guidance (see the Basic Set library's Machine.gct for the same 0-point treatment of these same facts), not separately purchased advantages.\n\nLore: A Necron does not need food, water, air or rest, and the Tomb Worlds' sixty-million-year sleep is exactly this: a body that simply does not wear down the way a living one does, left inert rather than fed or rested.\n\nDesign: 0 points, documentation only; see notes for why this is not a priced Doesn't Breathe/Doesn't Eat or Drink purchase.",
					"tags": [
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 0
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TlrVI0DwepldcF_rI",
			"name": "Body Lens: Deathmark",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Deathmarks are the dynasty's snipers and infiltrators, phased out of reality between shots and reappearing to deliver a single, precise kill. Codex background is explicit that a Deathmark carries far more individual initiative than a Warrior, but is still built as a specialised tool rather than a full personality.\n\nDesign: 252 (Attribute Modifiers) + 300 (Necrodermis DR 60, 5/level) = 552 points, added to Necron Physiology's 174 for 726 points of physiology and body alone. See the Deathmark template.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tM9ECepud058T1EbW",
					"name": "Attribute Modifiers (Deathmark)",
					"local_notes": "Design: Framework target: ST 26, DX 13, IQ 9, HT 14, HP 32. Increased ST +16 (160) for ST 26. Increased DX +3 (B15, 20/level, 60) for DX 13 -- a hunter-pattern chassis built for marksmanship over brute plating. Decreased IQ -1 (B16, 20/level, -20) for IQ 9 -- sharper than an Immortal, enough for solo infiltration work. Increased HT +4 (40) for HT 14. Extra Hit Points +6 (12) on top of the ST-26 base of 26, for HP 32. 160+60-20+40+12 = 252.",
					"tags": [
						"Physical"
					],
					"base_points": 252,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "st",
							"amount": 16
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "dx",
							"amount": 3
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "ht",
							"amount": 4
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "hp",
							"amount": 6
						},
						{
							"type": "attribute_bonus",
							"attribute": "iq",
							"amount": -1
						}
					]
				},
				{
					"id": "tkxWuI3yecAXo2ZPB",
					"name": "Necrodermis (Deathmark)",
					"reference": "B46",
					"local_notes": "DR 60, all locations. Weak Points 4 (a Warrior-pattern base chassis lightened for stealth, per framework.md): on a hit that fails to penetrate, roll 3d; 4 or less halves DR (30). Eye-slit optics (including the targeting lens) run at DR 30 rather than 60.\n\nLore: Necrodermis is a Necron's body and its armour at once: a skin of near-indestructible living metal with no separate suit to strip away. Universal across Codex background and every piece of Black Library Necron fiction.\n\nDesign: Damage Resistance (B46, 5/level, all locations): natural DR bought directly on the body, per framework.md's explicit instruction that necrodermis is a character trait, not equipment. Each body-type lens overrides `levels`/`dr` to its own figure from framework.md's Necron anchor table; see that lens's own copy of this trait for its point cost.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"points_per_level": 5,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 60,
					"features": [
						{
							"type": "dr_bonus",
							"locations": [
								"all"
							],
							"amount": 60
						}
					]
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "T3NZhV-5hYuUOxVCD",
			"name": "Mind: Deathmark Focus",
			"local_notes": "Lore: Deathmarks keep just enough of a mind to hunt alone and adapt to a single target, and nothing resembling a social self beyond that.\n\nDesign: 5 (Single-Minded) - 10 (No Sense of Humor) = -5 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Disadvantage",
				"Mental",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "tFn9GL7h2PiaO6oY9",
					"name": "Single-Minded",
					"reference": "B85",
					"local_notes": "Represents a Deathmark's total, undistractable focus on its assigned target.\n\nLore: A Deathmark exists to track and kill one target with total commitment, undistracted by anything else on the battlefield -- the defining trait of the caste across Codex background.\n\nDesign: Basic Set Single-Minded (B85), 5 points, the same entry this project's Astartes Hypno-Indoctrination package uses.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 5
				},
				{
					"id": "tkvmRSjL84xTTy9V-",
					"name": "No Sense of Humor",
					"reference": "B146",
					"local_notes": "Lore: A Deathmark has essentially no personality left to banter with -- minimal, cold, purpose-built.\n\nDesign: Basic Set No Sense of Humor (B146), -10 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Disadvantage",
						"Mental",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": -10
				}
			]
		},
		{
			"id": "TAjaO145WKk_bg4jJ",
			"name": "Deathmark Phase-Shift",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Deathmark hunts from phase: partly out of step with reality, it arrives unseen and turns a shot aside as it lands.\n\nDesign: 12 (Ethereal Interception) + 6 (Phase-In Ambush) = 18 points.",
			"tags": [
				"Advantage",
				"Physical",
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"container_type": "meta_trait",
			"children": [
				{
					"id": "t2F5DxhgeYjNkoKSq",
					"name": "Ethereal Interception",
					"reference": "B51",
					"local_notes": "+1 to Dodge against ranged attacks: the Deathmark phases partly out of reality as a shot arrives.\n\nLore: Deathmarks are described in the Codex as partially phased out of reality between shots, which is why fire aimed at them seems to pass through the moment before it lands.\n\nDesign: Enhanced Dodge (B51, 15/level) level 1, Limited to ranged attacks (-20%): 15 x 0.8 = 12 points.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"modifiers": [
						{
							"id": "mHks1EhMt5S48fWo7",
							"name": "Limited (ranged attacks only)",
							"cost_adj": "-20%"
						}
					],
					"points_per_level": 15,
					"can_level": true,
					"levels": 1
				},
				{
					"id": "tayW77vfg-N9pKF6H",
					"name": "Phase-In Ambush",
					"local_notes": "Once per scene, while out of sight of every enemy, the Deathmark may appear at any spot within 20 yards that it could see if it were standing there. It cannot move through solid barriers, and acts normally next turn.\n\nLore: Deathmarks are described arriving from the ambush position of a dimensional pocket or phase-shift and opening fire with no warning, rather than marching to a firing position.\n\nDesign: House price of 6 points, since the Basic Set library has no teleportation trait; one use per scene, out of sight only.",
					"tags": [
						"Advantage",
						"Physical",
						"Xenos",
						"Necrons"
					],
					"base_points": 6
				}
			]
		}
	],
	"skills": [
		{
			"id": "sbYMnDXwf0hcm64eW",
			"name": "Beam Weapons",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Deathmark's synaptic disintegrator is built for one perfect shot, not sustained fire.\n\nDesign: Beam Weapons (Rifle) (DX/E), the same family as the Warrior and Immortal templates. DX+5 (18) for 16 points, Easy.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"specialization": "Rifle",
			"difficulty": "dx/e",
			"points": 16
		},
		{
			"id": "syjcQxpDuAzcMUPop",
			"name": "Stealth",
			"local_notes": "Lore: A Deathmark's whole purpose is to be somewhere it shouldn't be able to reach, unnoticed, until it's too late.\n\nDesign: DX+2 (15) for 8 points, Average.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Necrons"
			],
			"difficulty": "dx/a",
